1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,825
An audit asks a plain question about a
release: which commit did the reviewer

2
00:00:08,865 --> 00:00:13,417
approve? The engineer opens the pull request
and reads the ID of its last commit.

3
00:00:13,457 --> 00:00:16,364
Then she looks for that ID
on main. It is not there.

4
00:00:16,404 --> 00:00:21,070
The commit on main has the same change, a
different ID, and a committer that is not

5
00:00:21,110 --> 00:00:22,260
a person on the team.

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,314
Your CTO asks: where did
the reviewed commit go?

7
00:00:25,654 --> 00:00:29,629
Nowhere. It is still in the repository,
under the pull request's ref.

8
00:00:29,669 --> 00:00:33,876
It never reached main, because of which
button was pressed at merge time.

9
00:00:33,916 --> 00:00:37,541
This video is about the three
buttons and what each one writes.

10
00:00:37,581 --> 00:00:42,371
Keep the audit question in mind: one bit
answers it, and you'll measure that bit in

11
00:00:42,411 --> 00:00:42,894
the demo.

12
00:00:44,734 --> 00:00:48,621
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:48,661 --> 00:00:54,028
This video opens Module 22. In the pull
request videos, the end of the lifecycle was

14
00:00:54,068 --> 00:00:59,306
one word, "merged", and I said it was the
only transition that writes to a branch.

15
00:00:59,346 --> 00:01:01,237
Now we look at what it writes.

16
00:01:01,577 --> 00:01:06,306
GitHub offers three merge methods: create
a merge commit, squash and merge, rebase

17
00:01:06,346 --> 00:01:09,680
and merge. All three are
server-side operations of GitHub.

18
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:14,392
All three give the base branch, the branch
the pull request merges into, the same

19
00:01:14,432 --> 00:01:19,161
content. They differ in what the history
says about how the content got there, and

20
00:01:19,201 --> 00:01:22,011
that difference decides
what you can prove later.

21
00:01:22,351 --> 00:01:27,940
Two replays. labs/ch17/merge-methods.sh
performs the three local commands that

22
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:32,342
correspond to the buttons, in
three copies of one repository.

23
00:01:32,382 --> 00:01:38,404
Asha merges. Two differences from GitHub
are visible in the replay, and I will point

24
00:01:38,444 --> 00:01:44,322
at them: the committer here is Asha, where
GitHub records itself, and nothing here

25
00:01:44,362 --> 00:01:48,796
is signed. The author of a commit
is whoever wrote the change.

26
00:01:48,836 --> 00:01:52,476
The committer is whoever
created the commit object.

27
00:01:52,516 --> 00:01:58,466
labs/ch17/rebase-deviations.sh shows how
the rebase button differs from a local git

28
00:01:58,506 --> 00:02:01,425
rebase. Then the walkthrough of Lab 22.1.

29
00:02:01,765 --> 00:02:07,717
Labels: git merge --no-ff, git merge
--squash and git rebase are 🟡 CAUTION: they

30
00:02:07,757 --> 00:02:13,635
move or rewrite the current branch. The
three merge buttons are 🟡 as well: they

31
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:15,508
write to the base branch.

32
00:02:17,348 --> 00:02:18,760
After this video you can:

33
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:23,519
Say for each method what lands on the
base branch and which commit IDs are new.

34
00:02:23,859 --> 00:02:26,935
Say who the author and the committer
of the resulting commits are.

35
00:02:27,275 --> 00:02:30,899
Show that the three results have the
same tree and different ancestry.

36
00:02:31,239 --> 00:02:35,142
Explain what the contributor's local
branch looks like after each method.

37
00:02:35,482 --> 00:02:39,252
Explain why "rebase and merge"
differs from a local git rebase.

38
00:02:41,092 --> 00:02:45,462
In one sentence: the three merge buttons
write three different histories for the

39
00:02:45,502 --> 00:02:49,982
same content: a merge commit that keeps
your commits, one new commit that replaces

40
00:02:50,022 --> 00:02:53,014
them, or new copies of your
commits in a straight line.

41
00:02:53,354 --> 00:02:58,072
Create a merge commit, a commit with two
parents. The documented mechanics: "all

42
00:02:58,112 --> 00:03:02,949
commits from the feature branch are added
to the base branch in a merge commit ...

43
00:03:02,989 --> 00:03:07,053
using the --no-ff option". New commits
on the base: one merge commit.

44
00:03:07,093 --> 00:03:12,048
Your original commit IDs on the base:
preserved. Your commits are unchanged, and the

45
00:03:12,088 --> 00:03:16,806
merge commit is committed by GitHub. Your
commits keep their signatures, and the

46
00:03:16,846 --> 00:03:20,375
merge commit is signed by GitHub.
The documented loss: none.

47
00:03:20,715 --> 00:03:25,616
Squash and merge. "The pull request's
commits are squashed into a single commit",

48
00:03:25,656 --> 00:03:30,130
then merged "using the fast-forward
option". New commits on the base: one.

49
00:03:30,170 --> 00:03:35,132
Your original commit IDs: not present. It
is committed by GitHub, and GitHub signs

50
00:03:35,172 --> 00:03:39,646
the final squash commit. The documented
loss, in GitHub's words: "You lose

51
00:03:39,686 --> 00:03:44,588
information about when specific changes
were originally made and who authored the

52
00:03:44,628 --> 00:03:45,686
squashed commits."

53
00:03:46,026 --> 00:03:50,890
Rebase and merge. Commits "are added onto
the base branch individually without a

54
00:03:50,930 --> 00:03:54,813
merge commit". New commits on the
base: one per original commit.

55
00:03:54,853 --> 00:03:58,123
Your original IDs: not present.
The method "always ...

56
00:03:58,163 --> 00:04:02,537
creates new commit SHAs". It "always
updates the committer information".

57
00:04:02,577 --> 00:04:07,318
Signature on what lands: none. The commits
are added "without commit signature

58
00:04:07,358 --> 00:04:10,751
verification". And originally
empty commits are dropped.

59
00:04:11,091 --> 00:04:15,073
Two rows connect to the rules of the
last three videos of this batch.

60
00:04:15,113 --> 00:04:19,037
Under a linear history rule, the
merge commit method is not allowed.

61
00:04:19,077 --> 00:04:23,117
Squash and rebase are, and rebase
and merge is limited to 100 commits.

62
00:04:23,457 --> 00:04:27,957
Why can rebase and merge not be signed?
In GitHub's words: "GitHub creates a

63
00:04:27,997 --> 00:04:32,975
modified commit ... GitHub didn't truly
create this commit, and can't therefore sign

64
00:04:33,015 --> 00:04:37,873
it as a generic system user. GitHub doesn't
have access to the committer's private

65
00:04:37,913 --> 00:04:42,772
signing keys." The documented workaround
is "to rebase and merge locally, and then

66
00:04:42,812 --> 00:04:47,551
push". You saw the mechanism in the signing
videos: a rewritten commit keeps its

67
00:04:47,591 --> 00:04:49,403
author and loses its signature.

68
00:04:49,743 --> 00:04:54,718
One caveat the textbook marks unverified:
who is recorded as the author of a squash

69
00:04:54,758 --> 00:04:59,794
commit, and whether other contributors become
Co-authored-by trailers, is not stated

70
00:04:59,834 --> 00:05:04,689
on the live documentation pages. Lab 22.1
has you read the real commit and record

71
00:05:04,729 --> 00:05:05,535
what you find.

72
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:10,460
Now the sentence to remember, in bold in
the book, with our branch on screen: the

73
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:14,000
merge method does not change what
the code is after the merge.

74
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:17,540
It changes what the history says
about how the code got there.

75
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:23,118
Quick quiz, with the audit from the
hook. Which button leaves the commit the

76
00:05:23,158 --> 00:05:27,354
reviewer approved on main, under
its own ID? A: merge commit.

77
00:05:27,394 --> 00:05:29,716
B: squash. C: rebase. Your answer?

78
00:05:34,556 --> 00:05:39,251
A. Only the merge commit method leaves the
reviewed commits on the base branch under

79
00:05:39,291 --> 00:05:43,930
their IDs. For you as contributor: your
local branch is an ancestor of main, so Git

80
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:45,001
knows it is merged.

81
00:05:45,341 --> 00:05:49,978
After a squash, main now has one new commit,
and your original commits are not part

82
00:05:50,018 --> 00:05:50,824
of its history.

83
00:05:51,164 --> 00:05:54,402
Once the head branch is
deleted, they are on no branch.

84
00:05:54,442 --> 00:05:56,668
They are still under refs/pull/N/head.

85
00:05:57,008 --> 00:06:01,939
After a rebase, the label main now sits on
three new copies, and the same holds for

86
00:06:01,979 --> 00:06:06,911
your originals. For the contributor, after
a squash or a rebase the local branch is

87
00:06:06,951 --> 00:06:11,163
not an ancestor of main, and git
branch -d will say "not fully merged".

88
00:06:11,203 --> 00:06:16,135
That is expected under those methods, and
the next video shows how to verify before

89
00:06:16,175 --> 00:06:16,674
deleting.

90
00:06:17,014 --> 00:06:20,132
How does the rebase button
differ from a local rebase?

91
00:06:20,172 --> 00:06:23,759
GitHub's rebase and merge "deviates
slightly from git rebase".

92
00:06:23,799 --> 00:06:28,614
It always updates the committer information
and creates new commit IDs, whereas git

93
00:06:28,654 --> 00:06:33,293
rebase does not change anything when the
branch already sits on top of the base.

94
00:06:33,333 --> 00:06:37,739
And it drops commits that were empty to
begin with, whereas git rebase keeps

95
00:06:37,779 --> 00:06:42,652
originally empty commits by default. So
after "Rebase and merge" the commits on main

96
00:06:42,692 --> 00:06:46,805
are never the commits you pushed,
even when no rebase seemed necessary.

97
00:06:47,145 --> 00:06:52,142
Who chooses? A repository's settings decide
which methods are offered, and a ruleset

98
00:06:52,182 --> 00:06:57,058
can restrict them per branch. With a merge
queue, "you no longer get to choose the

99
00:06:57,098 --> 00:07:00,057
merge method, as this is
controlled by the queue".

100
00:07:01,897 --> 00:07:06,261
The textbook's analogy: three ways
to file a report written in drafts.

101
00:07:06,301 --> 00:07:11,169
Staple the drafts into the folder with
a cover note: that is the merge commit.

102
00:07:11,209 --> 00:07:16,392
Retype one clean page: that is squash.
Retype every draft in order: that is rebase.

103
00:07:16,432 --> 00:07:21,614
The folder's content is the same. What you
can prove later about the drafts is not.

104
00:07:21,954 --> 00:07:27,582
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Three
columns: staple, one clean page, every

105
00:07:27,622 --> 00:07:33,042
page retyped. Under each, write how many
new commits main gets from a branch of

106
00:07:33,082 --> 00:07:34,217
three. I'll wait.

107
00:07:39,057 --> 00:07:44,311
One, one, and three. And only the first
column keeps the three originals as well.

108
00:07:44,651 --> 00:07:49,714
The analogy breaks because retyped pages
in Git are new objects with new IDs, and

109
00:07:49,754 --> 00:07:52,863
every tool that tracked
the old IDs has lost them.

110
00:07:53,203 --> 00:07:57,052
Hold on to the word "retyped".
Two of the three methods retype.

111
00:07:57,092 --> 00:08:02,051
Anything attached to the original pages
by their ID does not follow: a reviewer's

112
00:08:02,091 --> 00:08:06,248
approval, a check result, a signature,
your local branch's ancestry.

113
00:08:08,088 --> 00:08:14,483
On the left, the merge commit. Your three
commits, 44c1e7b, 12ae95d and 16d4788, are

114
00:08:14,523 --> 00:08:20,840
part of main's history, and one new commit,
4c53f2e, ties them in with two parents.

115
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:25,389
In the middle, squash. main gains one
commit, da48bba, with one parent.

116
00:08:25,429 --> 00:08:30,297
Your three commits are in brackets underneath:
they stay on the old branch, not on

117
00:08:30,337 --> 00:08:30,596
main.

118
00:08:30,936 --> 00:08:35,986
On the right, rebase. main gains three
commits in a straight line, with three new

119
00:08:36,026 --> 00:08:39,128
IDs. Your three originals
are, again, not on main.

120
00:08:39,468 --> 00:08:44,066
Now look for 16d4788, the commit the
reviewer approved, in each picture.

121
00:08:44,106 --> 00:08:46,384
It is on main in the first one only.

122
00:08:48,224 --> 00:08:50,745
The same starting point
in all three copies.

123
00:08:51,085 --> 00:08:54,381
Your branch has three commits,
and main has moved on by one.

124
00:08:54,721 --> 00:08:55,472
Method 1.

125
00:08:55,812 --> 00:08:56,557
Method 2.

126
00:08:56,897 --> 00:09:02,186
git merge --squash stops before committing
and says so: "Squash commit -- not

127
00:09:02,226 --> 00:09:06,615
updating HEAD". The ordinary commit
that follows has one parent.

128
00:09:06,955 --> 00:09:07,743
Method 3.

129
00:09:08,083 --> 00:09:11,926
A rebase of the pull request's commits
onto main, then a fast-forward.

130
00:09:14,266 --> 00:09:19,148
Before the comparison: for each copy, how
many commits did main gain, and how many

131
00:09:19,188 --> 00:09:22,569
of them have IDs that you, the
contributor, already know?

132
00:09:22,909 --> 00:09:27,608
Merge: four commits, three of them yours
under their own IDs, plus 4c53f2e.

133
00:09:27,648 --> 00:09:32,725
Squash: one commit, da48bba, and none of
yours. Rebase: three commits with you as

134
00:09:32,765 --> 00:09:37,653
author and Asha as committer, and three
new IDs. Read the author and committer

135
00:09:37,693 --> 00:09:42,644
columns: in the rebase copy they differ.
On GitHub, the committer of the squash

136
00:09:42,684 --> 00:09:46,751
commit, of the rebased commits and
of the merge commit is GitHub.

137
00:09:46,791 --> 00:09:51,994
Here it is Asha. That is the first difference
between this replay and the platform.

138
00:09:52,034 --> 00:09:54,206
The second: nothing here is signed.

139
00:09:56,546 --> 00:10:00,239
Three different histories. Will
the three tree IDs be equal?

140
00:10:00,579 --> 00:10:06,124
Identical: beff5a6 in all three copies.
The tree is the snapshot itself, the full

141
00:10:06,164 --> 00:10:11,020
listing of files. It is also the tree
of the test merge from video 123.

142
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:13,847
The code is the same. The history is not.

143
00:10:14,187 --> 00:10:19,671
What git log --first-parent shows on main:
one line per pull request for merge and

144
00:10:19,711 --> 00:10:23,848
for squash, one line per commit
for rebase. Now the audit bit.

145
00:10:23,888 --> 00:10:29,373
Is your original head commit an ancestor
of main? Predict the three exit statuses.

146
00:10:29,413 --> 00:10:30,586
Say them out loud.

147
00:10:35,426 --> 00:10:39,502
Exit status 0 for the merge commit,
1 for squash, 1 for rebase.

148
00:10:39,542 --> 00:10:44,860
That one bit is the answer to the audit
question from the hook, and it is what git

149
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:45,905
branch -d tests.

150
00:10:46,245 --> 00:10:50,147
A branch that already sits on top
of main, with one empty commit.

151
00:10:52,487 --> 00:10:55,570
Nothing has landed on main
since the branch was created.

152
00:10:55,610 --> 00:10:58,750
What does a local git rebase
main do to the four commits?

153
00:10:59,090 --> 00:11:04,274
Nothing: "Current branch pr-1 is up
to date." Same IDs, same committer.

154
00:11:04,614 --> 00:11:08,681
To imitate the button you must
force new commits: --no-ff.

155
00:11:08,721 --> 00:11:11,796
Four new IDs, and the
committer is now Asha.

156
00:11:12,136 --> 00:11:16,708
And with --no-keep-empty, the empty
commit "Trigger CI again" is gone: three

157
00:11:16,748 --> 00:11:21,563
commits. Those two options together are
what the documentation describes for the

158
00:11:21,603 --> 00:11:21,988
button.

159
00:11:22,328 --> 00:11:27,069
Read the state table before the walkthrough.
Any of the three merge buttons leaves

160
00:11:27,109 --> 00:11:31,909
your clone unchanged until you fetch. After
squash and rebase your original commits

161
00:11:31,949 --> 00:11:34,649
are on no branch, still
under refs/pull/N/head.

162
00:11:34,989 --> 00:11:39,735
Open three small pull requests and merge
each with a different method from the merge

163
00:11:39,775 --> 00:11:44,520
box. The control that performs the merge
has a menu that offers the enabled methods.

164
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:49,248
After each merge, fetch and read the facts
from your terminal, with the commands of

165
00:11:49,288 --> 00:11:49,875
the replay:

166
00:11:50,215 --> 00:11:54,983
Replace pr-head-commit with the ID of the
pull request's last commit, read from your

167
00:11:55,023 --> 00:11:59,391
own branch. For each merge, record four
things: how many commits main gained,

168
00:11:59,431 --> 00:12:04,142
whether your IDs are among them, who the
committer is, and whether the commit shows

169
00:12:04,182 --> 00:12:08,778
as verified on the page. For the squash
commit, also record who the author is and

170
00:12:08,818 --> 00:12:13,472
whether there are Co-authored-by trailers:
that is the item the documentation does

171
00:12:13,512 --> 00:12:16,220
not state, and your
observation is the evidence.

172
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:19,708
Five mistakes to watch for.

173
00:12:20,048 --> 00:12:24,650
Looking for the reviewed commit ID on
main after a squash or rebase merge.

174
00:12:24,690 --> 00:12:28,978
Root cause: both methods create new
commits; the originals stay under

175
00:12:29,018 --> 00:12:30,923
refs/pull/N/head, on no branch.

176
00:12:31,263 --> 00:12:34,567
Requiring signed commits and
offering "Rebase and merge".

177
00:12:34,607 --> 00:12:39,496
Root cause: GitHub cannot sign the rewritten
commits, so they land without signature

178
00:12:39,536 --> 00:12:40,258
verification.

179
00:12:40,598 --> 00:12:45,281
Concluding from git branch -d saying "not
fully merged" that the merge failed.

180
00:12:45,321 --> 00:12:50,064
Root cause: after squash or rebase the
local branch is not an ancestor of main,

181
00:12:50,104 --> 00:12:51,881
although its content is there.

182
00:12:52,221 --> 00:12:56,935
Assuming "Rebase and merge" keeps commits
unchanged when the branch is already up to

183
00:12:56,975 --> 00:13:01,123
date. Root cause: the button always
creates new commit IDs and updates the

184
00:13:01,163 --> 00:13:01,689
committer.

185
00:13:02,029 --> 00:13:06,981
Judging the methods by the resulting code.
Root cause: the tree of main is identical

186
00:13:07,021 --> 00:13:10,190
under all three; they differ
in ancestry and identity.

187
00:13:12,030 --> 00:13:16,645
Now, out of the lab. A payments team has
two requirements on main: commits must be

188
00:13:16,685 --> 00:13:21,357
signed, and an auditor must be able to see
that the reviewed commit is the deployed

189
00:13:21,397 --> 00:13:21,754
commit.

190
00:13:22,094 --> 00:13:25,127
Walk the three methods
against the two requirements.

191
00:13:25,167 --> 00:13:28,791
Rebase and merge fails the first:
what lands has no signature.

192
00:13:28,831 --> 00:13:33,756
Squash and merge passes the first, because
GitHub signs the squash commit, and fails

193
00:13:33,796 --> 00:13:38,306
the second literally: the commit on main
is not the commit that was reviewed.

194
00:13:38,346 --> 00:13:43,093
The link between them is the pull request,
a GitHub object, plus the pull request

195
00:13:43,133 --> 00:13:47,821
ref. The merge commit method passes both:
the reviewed commits are on main under

196
00:13:47,861 --> 00:13:52,431
their IDs with their own signatures, and
the merge commit is signed by GitHub.

197
00:13:52,771 --> 00:13:57,226
The chapter's edge cases put it in one
line: if compliance needs "the reviewed

198
00:13:57,266 --> 00:14:02,067
commit is the deployed commit", only the
merge-commit method satisfies it literally.

199
00:14:02,107 --> 00:14:06,735
The team allows only that method on main,
in the repository settings, and accepts

200
00:14:06,775 --> 00:14:10,250
the cost: a non-linear history,
and merge commits in the log.

