00:14:58,803 --> 00:15:03,614
Answer out loud. A strong answer takes the
two requirements one at a time and tests

212
00:15:03,654 --> 00:15:07,764
each method against each, with the
documented reason for every failure.

213
00:15:07,804 --> 00:15:11,212
It distinguishes "the same
content" from "the same commit".

214
00:15:11,252 --> 00:15:16,005
It ends with a recommendation, the setting
that enforces it, and the cost the team

215
00:15:16,045 --> 00:15:16,472
accepts.

216
00:15:18,312 --> 00:15:23,261
Let's land this. Same code under all three
buttons, three different histories, and

217
00:15:23,301 --> 00:15:26,242
you can now say what each
history lets you prove.

218
00:15:26,582 --> 00:15:28,242
You should now be able to say:

219
00:15:28,582 --> 00:15:32,656
A merge commit keeps your commits and
their IDs and adds one commit with two

220
00:15:32,696 --> 00:15:33,089
parents.

221
00:15:33,429 --> 00:15:37,923
A squash puts one new commit on the
base, with no ancestry to your branch.

222
00:15:38,263 --> 00:15:43,023
Rebase and merge puts new copies of each
commit on the base, always with new IDs and

223
00:15:43,063 --> 00:15:45,310
a new committer, and without signatures.

224
00:15:45,650 --> 00:15:49,980
The tree of the base branch is the same
under all three; only the merge commit

225
00:15:50,020 --> 00:15:53,062
method makes the reviewed
head an ancestor of the base.

226
00:15:53,402 --> 00:15:58,829
The button's rebase always rewrites and
drops empty commits; a local rebase onto an

227
00:15:58,869 --> 00:16:00,475
ancestor changes nothing.

228
00:16:02,315 --> 00:16:10,991
Read section 17.8 of Chapter 17. Do Exercise
22.1, "The three buttons from memory",

229
00:16:11,031 --> 00:16:14,246
in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

230
00:16:14,586 --> 00:16:18,545
That's the merge button, taken
apart. Practise with Lab 22.1.

231
00:16:18,585 --> 00:16:23,133
